                <pb facs="0262" n="164" xml:id="pg164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/11064: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-08-06T17:00:00+08:00">August 6, 1930—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 6—1:55 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>673. Legation’s 667, August 4, 5 p.m. Following from Commander in Chief, August
                    4th: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Palos</hi> restates [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">reports?</hi>] 2 Chinese, 1 British and 1
                            Japanese gunboat engaged with Communists at Changsha yesterday. This
                            morning a number of Ho Chien troops returned to southern end of city.
                            There was some firing throughout the night. There are 300 Japanese
                            present at northern end of city.</p>
                        <p>The Commander, Yangtze Patrol, now anchored off the Standard Oil Company,
                            will take no part in military operations for the recapture of troops
                                [<hi rendition="#italic">city?</hi>]. The U. S. S. <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guam</hi> is proceeding with stores and
                            coal.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
